nodecode-windows-eventlog is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 5bad3fb2baf04ce6befecfefce6d14f4c9c98934
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the three model-facing tools (win_crash_events,
win_shutdown_events, win_eventlog_query) with their arguments and their
JSON answer shapes; the Application log's crash family (Application Error
1000, Application Hang 1002, Windows Error Reporting 1001, .NET Runtime
1023/1026) and the provider check that keeps another provider's reuse of
those ids from being reported as a crash; the System log's machine-level
family (Kernel-Power 41, WER-SystemErrorReporting 1001, EventLog 6008,
User32 1074 and the 6005/6006/12/13 markers); the exception-code and
bugcheck-code tables with the decoding the original did (a decimal 159 and
a hex 0x9f both landing on DRIVER_POWER_STATE_FAILURE); the grouping of
repeated crashes by application and signature, busiest first; the window
walk that reads newest first and stops at the window's start or the scan
cap; the XPath the query is built from, with the channel and provider name
checks; the truncation limits and the case-blind field lookup that
tolerates the casing Windows builds disagree on; and the error shapes
(an ok=false answer rather than an exception, the exit code kept). The
Python is Common Lisp here: nlk:define-contract carries each tool's
arguments, nlk:run-bounded runs the read, and xml.lisp is a small XML
reader. Added, with no counterpart in the original: /windows-eventlog and
the manual topic, which read one channel into the lines an operator wants
first.

Left out: ctypes and the in-process evtapi calls (EvtQuery, EvtNext,
EvtRender, EvtClose) and the streaming iteration over them, replaced by one
wevtutil call per read, capped at scan_cap records, and the XML reader
that parses what it renders; the plugin's manifest and packaging
(plugin.yaml, pip/entry-point wiring), since a cell is a folder; and its
Hermes-specific tool-context arguments, which a Nodecode tool body does not
take. The String-versus-locale behaviour of provider text and the read-only
posture are unchanged: this queries, it never writes, clears or configures
a log.
